DTSTART:20261107T143000Z
DTEND:20261107T153000Z
LOCATION:MORRISON HALL
GEO:39.165496;-86.519719
SUMMARY:Heim Colloquium: "Who owns translations? On Literary Rights and C
 ultural Ownership"
DESCRIPTION:The Heim Colloquium is made possible by a generous donation t
 o Indiana University by the family of Michael Henry Heim (1943 – 2012)\
 , one of the most eminent literary translators of the late twentieth- and
  early twenty-first century in the US. The Heim bequest allows Indiana Un
 iversity to organize events on themes relevant to the many facets of Mich
 ael Henry Heim's work. For our inaugural colloquia\, we chose to focus on
  the imagined reader of literary translations (in 2024) and translator pa
 ratexts\, the introductions and afterwords translators have written about
  their work (in 2025). For this year's event\, we invited a core group of
  translation-focused graduate students at IU Bloomington to take the lead
  in forming the questions to be addressed and inviting a panel of experts
  to help us address them.  The title of this year's colloquium is "Who Ow
 ns Translation?"  Who owns translations? Is it the translator? The author
  of the source text? The publisher? The "foundation" and other estates th
 at control the rights? Our colloquium explores the question of ownership 
 from theoretical\, practical\, and speculative perspectives. First\, what
  are some ways that the translator conceptually "owns" the translation? A
 t issue are questions of how translators make choices that redistribute n
 arrative authority\, how they develop a responsibility for the work of an
 other\, and whether certain texts "invite" co-ownership while others do n
 ot.  We would also like to explore how the literary market shapes questio
 ns of ownership. How does the contemporary culture of self-promotion and 
 social media play a role in this ownership? What are some cultural imbala
 nces that exist when translating from minor languages and/or niche subcul
 tures into the dominant language and/or literary market? Additionally\, w
 hat practical considerations do translators have when obtaining rights of
  the source text and negotiating control over their translations? What in
 stitutional and legal barriers must one navigate? Are there creative loop
 holes to work around copyright and other regulations regarding translatio
 n?  Finally\, we want to offer a space for imagination. What would an ide
 al version of literary copyright look like?OUR SPEAKERS:Esther Allen     
  Esther Allen is a professor in the Ph.D. Programs in French\, Comparativ
 e Literature\, and Latin America\, Iberian and Latino Cultures (LAILaC) a
 t the City University of New York Graduate Center. Her translation of the
  Trilogy of Expectation by Antonio Di Benedetto (Zama\, The Silentiary\, 
 and The Suicides) was supported by a grant from the Guggenheim Foundation
  and Zama  was awarded the National Translation Award by ALTA. She has be
 en a Fellow at the Center for Scholars and Writers at the New York Public
  Library and was named a Chevalier de l'Ordre des arts et des lettres by 
 the French government. Her essays and translations have appeared in The N
 ew York Review of Books\, The LA Review of Books\, Words Without Borders\
 , The Paris Review\, The Poetry Foundation\, Nimrod\, and other publicati
 ons. She is currently completing a biography of José Martí.&#160\;    S
 tine An Stine An is a poet\, translator\, and performer based in New York
  City. She holds a BA in Literature from Harvard and an MFA in Literary A
 rts from Brown\, and has received fellowships and grants from The Poetry 
 Project\, the PEN/Heim Translation Fund Grant\, Yaddo\, ALTA\, and the Na
 tional Endowment for the Arts. Her publications include Today's Morning V
 ocabulary by Yoo Heekyung (2025)\, Winter Night Rabbit Worries by Yoo Hee
 kyung (2026)\, and S_MMER CR_SH (2025). Their debut poetry collection\, B
 -Dragon Suite (2026)\, won the 2023 Nightboat Poetry Prize and is availab
 le from Nightboat Books. You can find her online at gregorspamsa.com (htt
 ps://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fgregorspams
 a.com%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmcalver%40iu.edu%7Cda9fe283257c4b68c88608df2
 a1b8107%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639275969750963194%7
 CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOi
 JXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=i8K%2F
 mFJo4j08Q8t%2BK%2FfKjEAOu6h0IGLALe4StxZ%2F5B0%3D&amp\;reserved=0).Soleil 
 DavidSoleil Davíd is a poet and translator from the Philippines. She is 
 a National Endowment for the Arts fellow\, a winner of the Don Carlos Pal
 anca Memorial Awards for Literature\, and has received support from both 
 the Bread Loaf Writers' and Translators' Conferences\, MacDowell\, and th
 e DC Commission on the Arts and Humanities\, among others. Her translatio
 n of Carlo Paulo Pacolor's short story collection The Compendium of Impos
 sible Objects is forthcoming from Tilted Axis Press.&#160\;Rachel Cordasc
 oRachel Cordasco founded the website SFinTranslation.com in 2016 and regu
 larly reviews speculative fiction in translation for Strange Horizons and
  other magazines. Her translations from the Italian of Clelia Farris's sc
 ience fiction have been published in Clarkesworld Magazine\, Future Scien
 ce Fiction Digest\, and elsewhere. Rachel's books on SFT are Out of This 
 World: Speculative Fiction in Translation From the Cold War to the New Mi
 llennium (University of Illinois Press) and Speculative Fiction in Transl
 ation From Around the World (University of Wales Press).Mordecai MartinMo
 rdecai Martin is a 5th generation New Yorker\, a writer\, and a translato
 r of Yiddish and Hebrew poetry\, prose\, and scripture. He is a 2026-2027
  Translation Fellow at the Yiddish Book Center in Amherst\, MA and holds 
 an MFA from Randolph College. His translation work can be seen in literar
 y journals such as Exchanges\, Turkoslavia\, ANMLY\, and Asymptote. He li
 ves in Mexico City with his wife\, child and Pharaoh-Let-My People-Go the
  cat.  &#160\;SPEAKERS' PRECIRCULATED STATEMENTS:Esther Allen        It's
  startling to be asked the same question in the same week by Indiana Univ
 ersity and the Anthropic Copyright Settlement Administrator. My answers t
 o the Administrator varied considerably\, depending on the translated boo
 k in question. Context is everything\, and for this IU symposium\, the qu
 estion becomes a different one\, not "Who Owns (Your) Translation?" but "
 Who Owns Translation (Itself— as creative practice\, cause\, academic a
 nd publishing field)?"              "Own" can be synonymous with "appropr
 iate\," "possess\," "control\," and "enslave." But it's a capacious verb\
 , and can also operate in a different register to mean "admit\," "acknowl
 edge\," "feel responsible for\," or even "value" or "rejoice in." The fir
 st sense\, associated with the conventional paradigm of fidelity\, positi
 ons translation as a product sold in the information marketplace\, owned 
 by whoever has the money and power to control it.              The second
  register of "own" brings to mind the counter-paradigm of intimacy recent
 ly proposed by Sophie Collins. "While 'fidelity' implies the presence of 
 a primary source of power\, 'intimacy' indicates a mutual\, consensual an
 d willing exchange between two or more subjects."1              An entire
  lifetime ago (my specific lifetime)\, Guy Debord warned of the "informat
 ion theorists…\," "vassals of the lords of technocratic feudalism."2 Ye
 t he maintained that words are "fundamentally alien" to power\; language 
 "contain(s) forces that can upset the most careful calculations." Poetry\
 , he concluded\, "thus depends on the richest possibilities for living." 
              Nobody can own translation. Subjectivities\, in intimate exc
 hange with others\, can take responsibility for saving humanity and its l
 anguages from the information machines.      Stine An        "Toward a Co
 rnucopia of Copies"              Notes: The structure of this essay is co
 pied from Julia Sanches's statement for the 2024 Heim Colloqium "They're 
 Not Children": Imagined Readers in Contemporary Literary Translation.    
           1. All writing is derivative. Everything is a copy of a copy. E
 ven the alphabet is a copy of an ox\, a house\, a throwing stick. I\, too
 \, am a copy.              2. Before the advent of printing technology in
  certain jurisdictions\, copies were created by hand\, letter-by-letter\,
  literally\, by highly-trained\, literate individuals\, sometimes free an
 d often not. To copy was to write.              3. As a noun\, copy comes
  from the Latin copia ("an abundance\, ample supply\, plenty"). From com 
 ("with") plus ops ("power\, wealth\, resources"). A copy\, then\, makes p
 ossible a copious opulence.              4. As a verb\, copy cops from th
 e Medieval Latin copiare ("to transcribe"). To copy was "to write an orig
 inal text many times." To copy is to multiply originals. Enter\, translat
 or.              5. According to the Copyright Alliance\, copyright provi
 des property rights to encourage authors "to create new works and to make
  those works available to the public to enjoy." Copyright supports innova
 tion.              6. Keaton\, Bale\, Clooney\, Affleck\, Pattinson—eac
 h Batman brings their own interpretation. Each performance is derivative 
 and its own original thing. Once Jeremy Tiang pointed this out\, I see tr
 anslators as actors.              7. The translator is the author of a tr
 anslation. Translators increase\, grow\, and multiply the possibilities o
 f the text. Translators are actors of texts\, but are they granted an aut
 hor-actor's authority?              8. Translators do a lot more than act
 . Translators promote\, collaborate\, strategize\, advocate\, move\, prof
 ess\, and wrangle to introduce texts into a new language and market. Tran
 slators remain undercompensated and undercredited.              9. Copyri
 ght\, when employed well\, increases abundance. More abundance for the tr
 anslator in credit and compensation. More abundance for the public and li
 terary culture. Copyright\, when employed wrong\, protects hoarded scarci
 ty.              10. Can you imagine not giving an actor credit for their
  work? Yet\, some publishers still deny translators their copyright and r
 efuse to put translator names on the front cover. Why?      Soleil David 
        The idealist in me wants to say that a work of literature in trans
 lation belongs to all readers of the source and target languages. After a
 ll\, all ideas\, words\, and turns of phrases do not come from the void\,
  but rather through writers' continued presence in the world\, that is\, 
 in their relations with other human beings. But that idealism is quickly 
 shattered by the fact that a translator has to eat. So\, too\, does a wri
 ter. And the business of publishing all over the world rarely allows a wr
 iter nor translator to eat. In some cases\, usually in the Global North\,
  an author is paid handsomely\, or at the very least fairly\, for the lab
 or of writing\, and is again paid licensing fees for the use of their wor
 k as the source of translations.              A translator is usually pai
 d once. There are royalties\, maybe.              In my career of underpa
 id\, thankless jobs\, being a translator is yet another iteration. But st
 rangely enough\, my translation pays more than my poetry\, but that's for
  another colloquium. Also\, very importantly\, I translate from a languag
 e in the Global South\, where the currency and economy are depressed more
  than partly because of the United States and the Global North's internat
 ional and monetary policies. I have the dubious distinction of being a tr
 anslator who will most probably get paid more\, in terms of purchasing po
 wer\, than my author.              Questions of history\, comparative sta
 ndards of living\, and labor come up when I wrestle with this issue. Pain
 ful glimpses of the long-simmering tensions between those of the homeland
  and those of the diaspora make themselves visible. What\, then\, is our 
 responsibility towards our authors\, as translators trying to make a livi
 ng in better-funded countries?      Rachel CordascoIn thinking about "who
  owns translations\," I'm reminded of Robin Myers's remark in an intervie
 w for the Booker Prizes website that "[t]he practice of reading\, writing
 \, and publishing translations reminds us\, and humbles us all in doing s
 o\, that every book is a collaborative and continually (re)generative cre
 ation\, expansive and refractive across time and space." A published tran
 slation can't happen without the author and the original text\, the trans
 lator and the translated text\, the editor\, and the publisher. Thinking 
 about "ownership" conceptually when it comes to translation\, though\, sh
 ifts the discussion. After all\, without the translator\, the translation
  wouldn't exist\, and the author would be left without their text being r
 ead by a wider audience\, and the editor and publisher wouldn't have a bo
 ok to work with. In this way\, I tend to see the translator as the "owner
 " of the translation because it is their creation. Ultimately\, the trans
 lator must take "ownership" of their work in terms of standing behind the
  choices they've made\, but once it's out in the world\, the text becomes
  a different kind of creature. Yes\, the translator remains the translato
 r\, but I like to think of this new relationship with readers as one of "
 creating" and "bestowing." A translation\, once it is in the world\, is c
 onsumed by the author of the original text\, the editor\, and readers\, a
 nd takes on a new life as a text in conversation with other texts. The tr
 anslator has brought it into another language\, and their name should be 
 clearly associated with that translation (on the cover\, on the title pag
 e\, in marketing copy\, etc.) because it is their work. How readers will 
 then accept it\, review it\, and discuss it\, though\, is nothing that th
 e translator can control. So I suppose that this discussion could veer in
 to issues of "control\," as well.Mordecai Martin        On a flight betwe
 en London and New York\, halfway over the Atlantic\, a man gets up from h
 is seat\, goes to the bathroom and emerges a terrifying visage. In a perf
 ect replica of an SS officer's uniform\, right down to the totenkopf insi
 gnia\, he wields an assault rifle he has somehow smuggled onto the plane.
         "Alright\," he snarls\, "who's a Jew?!"              "ווייס�
 �ו ואָס?"              Says the black hatted Hasid in the exit row to
  his similarly dressed companion\,              "ס׳איז אַ גוטע 
 פֿראַגע!"              "Y'know something? It's a good question!"    
     Does the joke change if the flight is from Brussels? Vilnius? Warsaw?
  Kyiv? St. Petersburg? Tel Aviv?        Yiddish has spent its modern hist
 ory as a language easier to locate in certain municipalities than inside 
 contested borders. Jewish identity remains almost as contested throughout
  its history as those borders. Those debates have always been religious\,
  political\, and deeply complicated. Thus the above joke. Did you get the
  joke? If so\, do you own the joke?        As a translator of Ukrainian-b
 orn immigrant New Yorker Yiddish cartoonist\, humorist\, puppeteer\, comm
 unist Yosl Cutler\, I have been constantly confronted with who owns his w
 orks. From hunts for heirs and copyright holders\, to glossing Cutler's p
 olitics and my own\, to debating whether or not finding information in pu
 blic Yiddish archives held by Israel violates a commitment to the Palesti
 nian Acadaemic and Cultural Boycott of Israel\, my project is rife with q
 uestions of what is Jewish humor and who gets to say that it is theirs/ou
 rs/yours/mine.        Cutler's book is called מונטערגאַנג\, a p
 unning title that plays on the words for cheerful and disaster. I render 
 it as MERRILY WE GO A-WRONG. Merrily go a-wrong with us?
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">
 The Heim Colloquium is made possible by a generous donation to Indiana Un
 iversity by the family of Michael Henry Heim (1943 – 2012)\, one of the
  most eminent literary translators of the late twentieth- and early twent
 y-first century in the US.</span> The Heim bequest allows Indiana Univers
 ity to organize events on themes relevant to the many facets of Michael H
 enry Heim's work. For our inaugural colloquia\, we chose to focus on the 
 imagined reader of literary translations (in 2024) and translator paratex
 ts\, the introductions and afterwords translators have written about thei
 r work (in 2025). For this year's event\, we invited a core group of tran
 slation-focused graduate students at IU Bloomington to take the lead in f
 orming the questions to be addressed and inviting a panel of experts to h
 elp us address them.</p><p>  The title of this year's colloquium is "Who 
 Owns Translation?"</p><p>  Who owns translations? Is it the translator? T
 he author of the source text? The publisher? The "foundation" and other e
 states that control the rights? Our colloquium explores the question of o
 wnership from theoretical\, practical\, and speculative perspectives. Fir
 st\, what are some ways that the translator conceptually "owns" the trans
 lation? At issue are questions of how translators make choices that redis
 tribute narrative authority\, how they develop a responsibility for the w
 ork of another\, and whether certain texts "invite" co-ownership while ot
 hers do not.</p><p>  We would also like to explore how the literary marke
 t shapes questions of ownership. How does the contemporary culture of sel
 f-promotion and social media play a role in this ownership? What are some
  cultural imbalances that exist when translating from minor languages and
 /or niche subcultures into the dominant language and/or literary market? 
 Additionally\, what practical considerations do translators have when obt
 aining rights of the source text and negotiating control over their trans
 lations? What institutional and legal barriers must one navigate? Are the
 re creative loopholes to work around copyright and other regulations rega
 rding translation?</p><p>  Finally\, we want to offer a space for imagina
 tion. What would an ideal version of literary copyright look like?</p><p>
 <strong>OUR SPEAKERS:</strong></p><div class="lw_accordion"><section clas
 s="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-
 expanded="false" type="button">Esther Allen</button></h4><div class="lw_a
 ccordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true">      Esther Allen is a profes
 sor in the Ph.D. Programs in French\, Comparative Literature\, and Latin 
 America\, Iberian and Latino Cultures (LAILaC) at the City University of 
 New York Graduate Center. Her translation of the Trilogy of Expectation b
 y Antonio Di Benedetto (<i>Zama\, The Silentiary\, </i>and <i>The Suicide
 s</i>) was supported by a grant from the Guggenheim Foundation and <i>Zam
 a </i> was awarded the National Translation Award by ALTA. She has been a
  Fellow at the Center for Scholars and Writers at the New York Public Lib
 rary and was named a Chevalier de l'Ordre des arts et des lettres by the 
 French government. Her essays and translations have appeared in <i>The Ne
 w York Review of Books\, The LA Review of Books\, Words Without Borders\,
  The Paris Review\, The Poetry Foundation\, Nimrod\, </i>and other public
 ations. She is currently completing a biography of José Martí.&#160\;  
