 d="true"/></picture>Mordecai Martin is a 5th generation New Yorker\, a wr
 iter\, and a translator of Yiddish and Hebrew poetry\, prose\, and script
 ure. He is a 2026-2027 Translation Fellow at the Yiddish Book Center in A
 mherst\, MA and holds an MFA from Randolph College. His translation work 
 can be seen in literary journals such as Exchanges\, Turkoslavia\, ANMLY\
 , and Asymptote. He lives in Mexico City with his wife\, child and Pharao
 h-Let-My People-Go the cat.</span><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody
 "><br /></span></div></section></div><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>SPEAKERS'
  PRECIRCULATED STATEMENTS:</strong></p><div class="lw_accordion"><section
  class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button 
 aria-expanded="false" type="button">Esther Allen</button></h4><div class=
 "lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><p>        It's startling
  to be asked the same question in the same week by Indiana University and
  the Anthropic Copyright Settlement Administrator. My answers to the Admi
 nistrator varied considerably\, depending on the translated book in quest
 ion. Context is everything\, and for this IU symposium\, the question bec
 omes a different one\, not "Who Owns (Your) Translation?" but "Who Owns T
 ranslation (Itself— as creative practice\, cause\, academic and publish
 ing field)?"      </p><p>        "Own" can be synonymous with "appropriat
 e\," "possess\," "control\," and "enslave." But it's a capacious verb\, a
 nd can also operate in a different register to mean "admit\," "acknowledg
 e\," "feel responsible for\," or even "value" or "rejoice in." The first 
 sense\, associated with the conventional paradigm of fidelity\, positions
  translation as a product sold in the information marketplace\, owned by 
 whoever has the money and power to control it.      </p><p>        The se
 cond register of "own" brings to mind the counter-paradigm of intimacy re
 cently proposed by Sophie Collins. "While 'fidelity' implies the presence
  of a primary source of power\, 'intimacy' indicates a mutual\, consensua
 l and willing exchange between two or more subjects."1      </p><p>      
   An entire lifetime ago (my specific lifetime)\, Guy Debord warned of th
 e "information theorists…\," "vassals of the lords of technocratic feud
 alism."2 Yet he maintained that words are "fundamentally alien" to power\
 ; language "contain(s) forces that can upset the most careful calculation
 s." Poetry\, he concluded\, "thus depends on the richest possibilities fo
 r living."      </p><p>        Nobody can own translation. Subjectivities
 \, in intimate exchange with others\, can take responsibility for saving 
 humanity and its languages from the information machines.      </p></div>
 </section><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_blo
 ck_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Stine An</button></
 h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><p>        
 "Toward a Cornucopia of Copies"      </p><p>        Notes: The structure 
 of this essay is copied from Julia Sanches's statement for the 2024 Heim 
 Colloqium "They're Not Children": Imagined Readers in Contemporary Litera
 ry Translation.      </p><p>        1. All writing is derivative. Everyth
 ing is a copy of a copy. Even the alphabet is a copy of an ox\, a house\,
  a throwing stick. I\, too\, am a copy.      </p><p>        2. Before the
  advent of printing technology in certain jurisdictions\, copies were cre
 ated by hand\, letter-by-letter\, literally\, by highly-trained\, literat
 e individuals\, sometimes free and often not. To copy was to write.      
 </p><p>        3. As a noun\, copy comes from the Latin copia ("an abunda
 nce\, ample supply\, plenty"). From com ("with") plus ops ("power\, wealt
 h\, resources"). A copy\, then\, makes possible a copious opulence.      
 </p><p>        4. As a verb\, copy cops from the Medieval Latin copiare (
 "to transcribe"). To copy was "to write an original text many times." To 
 copy is to multiply originals. Enter\, translator.      </p><p>        5.
  According to the Copyright Alliance\, copyright provides property rights
  to encourage authors "to create new works and to make those works availa
 ble to the public to enjoy." Copyright supports innovation.      </p><p> 
        6. Keaton\, Bale\, Clooney\, Affleck\, Pattinson—each Batman bri
 ngs their own interpretation. Each performance is derivative and its own 
 original thing. Once Jeremy Tiang pointed this out\, I see translators as
  actors.      </p><p>        7. The translator is the author of a transla
 tion. Translators increase\, grow\, and multiply the possibilities of the
  text. Translators are actors of texts\, but are they granted an author-a
 ctor's authority?      </p><p>        8. Translators do a lot more than a
 ct. Translators promote\, collaborate\, strategize\, advocate\, move\, pr
 ofess\, and wrangle to introduce texts into a new language and market. Tr
 anslators remain undercompensated and undercredited.      </p><p>        
 9. Copyright\, when employed well\, increases abundance. More abundance f
 or the translator in credit and compensation. More abundance for the publ
 ic and literary culture. Copyright\, when employed wrong\, protects hoard
 ed scarcity.      </p><p>        10. Can you imagine not giving an actor 
 credit for their work? Yet\, some publishers still deny translators their
  copyright and refuse to put translator names on the front cover. Why?   
    </p></div></section><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_
 accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Soleil
  David</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="
 true"><p>        The idealist in me wants to say that a work of literatur
 e in translation belongs to all readers of the source and target language
 s. After all\, all ideas\, words\, and turns of phrases do not come from 
 the void\, but rather through writers' continued presence in the world\, 
 that is\, in their relations with other human beings. But that idealism i
 s quickly shattered by the fact that a translator has to eat. So\, too\, 
 does a writer. And the business of publishing all over the world rarely a
 llows a writer nor translator to eat. In some cases\, usually in the Glob
 al North\, an author is paid handsomely\, or at the very least fairly\, f
 or the labor of writing\, and is again paid licensing fees for the use of
  their work as the source of translations.      </p><p>        A translat
 or is usually paid once. There are royalties\, maybe.      </p><p>       
  In my career of underpaid\, thankless jobs\, being a translator is yet a
 nother iteration. But strangely enough\, my translation pays more than my
  poetry\, but that's for another colloquium. Also\, very importantly\, I 
 translate from a language in the Global South\, where the currency and ec
 onomy are depressed more than partly because of the United States and the
  Global North's international and monetary policies. I have the dubious d
 istinction of being a translator who will most probably get paid more\, i
 n terms of purchasing power\, than my author.      </p><p>        Questio
 ns of history\, comparative standards of living\, and labor come up when 
 I wrestle with this issue. Painful glimpses of the long-simmering tension
 s between those of the homeland and those of the diaspora make themselves
  visible. What\, then\, is our responsibility towards our authors\, as tr
 anslators trying to make a living in better-funded countries?      </p></
 div></section><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion
 _block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Rachel Cordasco
 </button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true">
 <p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">In thinking about "who owns t
 ranslations\," I'm reminded of Robin Myers's remark in an interview for t
 he Booker Prizes website that "[t]he practice of reading\, writing\, and 
 publishing translations reminds us\, and humbles us all in doing so\, tha
 t every book is a collaborative and continually (re)generative creation\,
  expansive and refractive across time and space." A published translation
  can't happen without the author and the original text\, the translator a
 nd the translated text\, the editor\, and the publisher. Thinking about "
 ownership" conceptually when it comes to translation\, though\, shifts th
 e discussion. After all\, without the translator\, the translation wouldn
 't exist\, and the author would be left without their text being read by 
 a wider audience\, and the editor and publisher wouldn't have a book to w
 ork with. In this way\, I tend to see the translator as the "owner" of th
 e translation because it is their creation. Ultimately\, the translator m
 ust take "ownership" of their work in terms of standing behind the choice
 s they've made\, but once it's out in the world\, the text becomes a diff
 erent kind of creature. Yes\, the translator remains the translator\, but
  I like to think of this new relationship with readers as one of "creatin
 g" and "bestowing." A translation\, once it is in the world\, is consumed
  by the author of the original text\, the editor\, and readers\, and take
 s on a new life as a text in conversation with other texts. The translato
 r has brought it into another language\, and their name should be clearly
  associated with that translation (on the cover\, on the title page\, in 
 marketing copy\, etc.) because it is their work. How readers will then ac
 cept it\, review it\, and discuss it\, though\, is nothing that the trans
 lator can control. So I suppose that this discussion could veer into issu
 es of "control\," as well.</span></p></div></section><section class="lw_a
 ccordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expande
 d="false" type="button">Mordecai Martin</button></h4><div class="lw_accor
 dion_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><div data-olk-copy-source="Message
 Body">        On a flight between London and New York\, halfway over the 
 Atlantic\, a man gets up from his seat\, goes to the bathroom and emerges
  a terrifying visage. In a perfect replica of an SS officer's uniform\, r
 ight down to the totenkopf insignia\, he wields an assault rifle he has s
 omehow smuggled onto the plane.<br /><br />        "Alright\," he snarls\
 , "who's a Jew?!"      </div><div style="padding-left: 360px\;">        "
 ווייסטו ואָס?"      </div><div>        Says the black hatted Ha
 sid in the exit row to his similarly dressed companion\,      </div><div 
 style="padding-left: 320px\;">        "ס׳איז אַ גוטע פֿראַג
 ע!"      </div><div><br />        "Y'know something? It's a good questio
 n!"<br /><br />        Does the joke change if the flight is from Brussel
 s? Vilnius? Warsaw? Kyiv? St. Petersburg? Tel Aviv?<br /><br />        Yi
 ddish has spent its modern history as a language easier to locate in cert
 ain municipalities than inside contested borders. Jewish identity remains
  almost as contested throughout its history as those borders. Those debat
 es have always been religious\, political\, and deeply complicated. Thus 
 the above joke. Did you get the joke? If so\, do you own the joke?<br /><
 br />        As a translator of Ukrainian-born immigrant New Yorker Yiddi
 sh cartoonist\, humorist\, puppeteer\, communist Yosl Cutler\, I have bee
 n constantly confronted with who owns his works. From hunts for heirs and
  copyright holders\, to glossing Cutler's politics and my own\, to debati
 ng whether or not finding information in public Yiddish archives held by 
 Israel violates a commitment to the Palestinian Acadaemic and Cultural Bo
 ycott of Israel\, my project is rife with questions of what is Jewish hum
 or and who gets to say that it is theirs/ours/yours/mine.<br /><br /><br 
 />        Cutler's book is called מונטערגאַנג\, a punning title
  that plays on the words for cheerful and disaster. I render it as MERRIL
 Y WE GO A-WRONG. Merrily go a-wrong with us?      </div></div></section><
 /div>
