LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Estela Vieira
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) w
 elcomes Dr. Estela Vieira for Machado de Assis and the Female Reader\, as
  part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Tuesday\, Novembe
 r 10th | 4–5PM | Hamilton Lugar School | GA2067Abstract: Machado de Ass
 is and the Female Reader  Widely regarded as one of Brazil's greatest wri
 ters\, Joaquim Maria Machado de Assis (1839-1908) is known for directly a
 ddressing and implicating his readers\, but what happens when that reader
  is a woman and that dialogue turns into a critical force in his fiction?
  This presentation will first briefly summarize my broader research on wo
 men readers\, writers\, and literary agency in Machado de Assis\, surveyi
 ng women-led scholarship on Machado and the author's personal library and
  correspondence to show his concern for women's active role in Brazilian 
 literary culture. The core of the talk will focus on his penultimate nove
 l\, Esaú e Jacó (Esau and Jacob 1904)\, and analyze the ways in which t
 he narrator and female reader intersect and evolve in the novel. As she e
 merges as confidante\, adversary\, and ironic double of the protagonist\,
  Flora\, she also begins to usurp the narrator/writer. This development u
 nravels into a theorization of the relationship between literature and wo
 men (as readers\, writers\, characters)\, a topic Machado was keen to eng
 age with as he aligns reading with feminine critical agency.&#160\;Bio:  
 Dr. Estela Vieira is a scholar of Lusophone literary and cultural studies
  with special interests in nineteenth-century literature\, film\, and wom
 en writers. Her first book studied the function and representation of the
  interior space in three realist novels. She is currently working on a ma
 nuscript that studies female authorship in nineteenth-century Portugal. S
 he is also interested in film and has published articles on film and lite
 rature\, political memory and cinema\, and Iberian women filmmakers. Her 
 research has focused on nineteenth-century writers including Machado de A
 ssis\, Eça de Queirós\, Júlio Dinis\; on Portugal's historical memory 
 and cultural identity\; and on modernist poetics and contemporary authors
  such as José Saramago.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for L
 atin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes <strong>Dr. Estela V
 ieira</strong> for <strong><em>Machado de Assis and the Female Reader</em
 ></strong>\, as part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</
 p><p data-pm-slice="1 1 []"><strong>Tuesday\, November 10th | 4–5PM | H
 amilton Lugar School | GA2067</strong></p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong> M
 achado de Assis and the Female Reader<br />  Widely regarded as one of Br
 azil's greatest writers\, Joaquim Maria Machado de Assis (1839-1908) is k
 nown for directly addressing and implicating his readers\, but what happe
 ns when that reader is a woman and that dialogue turns into a critical fo
 rce in his fiction? This presentation will first briefly summarize my bro
 ader research on women readers\, writers\, and literary agency in Machado
  de Assis\, surveying women-led scholarship on Machado and the author's p
 ersonal library and correspondence to show his concern for women's active
  role in Brazilian literary culture. The core of the talk will focus on h
 is penultimate novel\, Esaú e Jacó (Esau and Jacob 1904)\, and analyze 
 the ways in which the narrator and female reader intersect and evolve in 
 the novel. As she emerges as confidante\, adversary\, and ironic double o
 f the protagonist\, Flora\, she also begins to usurp the narrator/writer.
  This development unravels into a theorization of the relationship betwee
 n literature and women (as readers\, writers\, characters)\, a topic Mach
 ado was keen to engage with as he aligns reading with feminine critical a
 gency.&#160\;</p><p><strong>Bio:</strong></p><p>  Dr. Estela Vieira is a 
 scholar of Lusophone literary and cultural studies with special interests
  in nineteenth-century literature\, film\, and women writers. Her first b
 ook studied the function and representation of the interior space in thre
 e realist novels. She is currently working on a manuscript that studies f
 emale authorship in nineteenth-century Portugal. She is also interested i
 n film and has published articles on film and literature\, political memo
 ry and cinema\, and Iberian women filmmakers. Her research has focused on
  nineteenth-century writers including Machado de Assis\, Eça de Queirós
 \, Júlio Dinis\; on Portugal's historical memory and cultural identity\;
  and on modernist poetics and contemporary authors such as José Saramago
 .</p><p><picture class="lw_image lw_image33455  lw_align_left"><source me
 dia="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/
 live/image/scale/2x/gid/137/width/500/height/669/33455_clacs_symposium_-_
 estela_vieira.rev.1793980828.webp 2x" data-origin="responsive"/><source m
 edia="(max-width: 500px)" type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu
 /live/image/scale/2x/gid/137/width/500/height/669/33455_clacs_symposium_-
 _estela_vieira.rev.1793980828.jpg 2x" data-origin="responsive"/><source m
 edia="(min-width: 501px)" type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu
