SUMMARY:CLACS Faculty Research Symposium with Nilesh Shinde
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) w
 elcomes Dr. Nilesh Shinde (O'Neill School of Public and Environmental Aff
 airs) for Land\, Policy\, and Documented Violence on Brazil's Amazon Fron
 tier\, part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.  Tuesday\,
  November 17th | 4-5PM | Hamilton Lugar School GA3067Abstract: Can rules 
 governing land and forests make people safer on Brazil's Amazon frontier?
  This talk brings together three studies of rural violence\, land registr
 ation\, and forest enforcement. Drawing on 24 years of records from Brazi
 l's Pastoral Land Commission\, I show that Indigenous people account for 
 a growing share of documented murder victims and examine how the geograph
 y of recorded violence has shifted. I then assess two policies: Pará's l
 and registration mandate\, which was followed by fewer recorded threats a
 nd conflicts\, and Brazil's priority municipality program\, which substan
 tially reduced deforestation. The evidence for the program's social benef
 its is less certain because estimates of violence depend on which municip
 alities are used for comparison. Together\, the studies show why evaluati
 ng environmental policy requires careful attention to both the limits of 
 violence records and the reasons places were selected for intervention.Bi
 o: Nilesh Shinde is an Assistant Professor at Indiana University Blooming
 ton's O'Neill School of Public and Environmental Affairs. An environmenta
 l economist and engineer\, he studies how property rights\, institutions\
 , and environmental policy shape land and water use. His research spans d
 eforestation in the Brazilian Amazon\, groundwater and irrigation in Indi
 a\, and water pollution in the United States.
