 s of records from Brazil's Pastoral Land Commission\, I show that Indigen
 ous people account for a growing share of documented murder victims and e
 xamine how the geography of recorded violence has shifted. I then assess 
 two policies: Pará's land registration mandate\, which was followed by f
 ewer recorded threats and conflicts\, and Brazil's priority municipality 
 program\, which substantially reduced deforestation. The evidence for the
  program's social benefits is less certain because estimates of violence 
 depend on which municipalities are used for comparison. Together\, the st
 udies show why evaluating environmental policy requires careful attention
  to both the limits of violence records and the reasons places were selec
